1	# MCP Tasks Organizer
2	[![smithery badge](https://smithery.ai/badge/@rohanpatriot/mcp-tasks-organizer)](https://smithery.ai/server/@rohanpatriot/mcp-tasks-organizer) [![Verified on MseeP](https://mseep.ai/badge.svg)](https://mseep.ai/app/4cba506f-04a5-422d-999d-6794c34ff81d) <a href="https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/@rohanpatriot/mcp-tasks-organizer">
3	  <img width="380" height="200" src="https://glama.ai/mcp/servers/@rohanpatriot/mcp-tasks-organizer/badge" />
4	</a>
5	
6	An MCP server that converts Cursor agent plans into structured markdown task lists and organizes them in your repository. This server helps you track AI-generated plans and recommendations as actionable specifications.
7	
8	## Features
9	
10	- Automatically extracts tasks from Cursor agent plans
11	- Creates a `.tasks` folder in your repository for organized task management
12	- Uses descriptive filenames (e.g., "refactor-authentication.md") for easy identification
13	- Automatically marks completed task lists with a ✅ prefix
14	- Formats plans with proper Markdown structure
15	- Organizes content into Overview, Implementation Details, and Next Steps
16	- Integrates with Claude for Desktop and other MCP clients
17	
18	## Installation
19	
20	### Installing via Smithery
21	
22	To install Tasks Organizer for Claude Desktop automatically via [Smithery](https://smithery.ai/server/@huntsyea/mcp-tasks-organizer):
23	
24	```bash
25	npx -y @smithery/cli install @huntsyea/mcp-tasks-organizer --client claude
26	```
27	
28	### Prerequisites
29	
30	- Python 3.10 or higher
31	- pip or another Python package manager
32	
33	### Quick Install
34	
35	For Unix-based systems (macOS, Linux):
36	
37	```bash
38	# Clone the repository
39	git clone https://github.com/yourusername/mcp-tasks-organizer.git
40	cd mcp-tasks-organizer
41	
42	# Run the installation script
43	./install.sh
44	```
45	
46	For Windows:
47	
48	```batch
49	# Clone the repository
50	git clone https://github.com/yourusername/mcp-tasks-organizer.git
51	cd mcp-tasks-organizer
52	
53	# Run the installation script
54	install.bat
55	```
56	
57	### Manual Installation
58	
59	```bash
60	# Clone the repository
61	git clone https://github.com/yourusername/mcp-tasks-organizer.git
62	cd mcp-tasks-organizer
63	
64	# Create and activate a virtual environment (optional but recommended)
65	python -m venv venv
66	source venv/bin/activate  # On Windows: venv\Scripts\activate
67	
68	# Install dependencies
69	pip install -r requirements.txt
70	
71	# Install the package
72	pip install -e .
73	```
74	
75	## Usage with Claude for Desktop
76	
77	1. Install Claude for Desktop from [claude.ai/download](https://claude.ai/download)
78	
79	2. Configure Claude for Desktop to use this MCP server:
80	
81	   Open `~/Library/Application Support/Claude/claude_desktop_config.json` (macOS) or `%APPDATA%\Claude\claude_desktop_config.json` (Windows) and add:
82	
83	   ```json
84	   {
85	     "mcpServers": {
86	       "tasks-organizer": {
87	         "command": "python",
88	         "args": ["-m", "tasks_organizer"]
89	       }
90	     }
91	   }
92	   ```
93	
94	3. Restart Claude for Desktop
95	
96	4. Use the server by asking Claude about your cursor plans, for example:
97	   - "Convert this cursor plan into a task list called 'Refactor Authentication' with description 'auth-refactor': [paste plan]"
98	   - "Create a markdown task list from this cursor agent output with title 'Database Migration' and description 'db-migration': [paste output]"
99	   - "Mark task 2 in the auth-refactor task list as complete"
100	   - "List all task files in my repository"
101	
102	## Available Tools
103	
104	The server provides these tools:
105	
106	### 1. create_task_list
107	
108	Create a new task list and save it to the .tasks folder.
109	
110	Parameters:
111	- `title`: Title for the task list
112	- `description`: Short 2-3 word description for the filename (e.g., "refactor-authentication") 
113	- `repo_path`: Path to the repository root (defaults to current directory)
114	- `include_metadata`: Whether to include creation date/time
115	
116	### 2. convert_plan_to_tasks
117	
118	Convert a Cursor agent's plan text into a formatted Markdown task list and save it.
119	
120	Parameters:
121	- `plan_text`: The plan text from the Cursor agent
122	- `title`: Title for the task list
123	- `description`: Short 2-3 word description for the filename (e.g., "refactor-authentication")
124	- `repo_path`: Path to the repository root (defaults to current directory)
125	- `include_metadata`: Whether to include metadata like date and time
126	
127	### 3. add_task
128	
129	Add a new task to an existing task list.
130	
131	Parameters:
132	- `description`: The description identifier of the task list file
133	- `task_text`: Text for the new task
134	- `repo_path`: Path to the repository root (defaults to current directory)
135	- `section`: Which section to add the task to (defaults to "Tasks")
136	
137	### 4. mark_task_complete
138	
139	Mark a specific task as completed.
140	
141	Parameters:
142	- `description`: The description identifier of the task list file
143	- `task_number`: The number of the task to mark as complete
144	- `repo_path`: Path to the repository root (defaults to current directory)
145	- `section`: Which section the task is in (defaults to "Tasks")
146	
147	### 5. check_all_tasks_complete
148	
149	Check if all tasks are complete and mark the task list as completed by renaming with ✅ prefix.
150	
151	Parameters:
152	- `description`: The description identifier of the task list file
153	- `repo_path`: Path to the repository root (defaults to current directory)
154	
155	### 6. list_task_files
156	
157	List all task files in the .tasks directory.
158	
159	Parameters:
160	- `repo_path`: Path to the repository root (defaults to current directory)
161	- `include_completed`: Whether to include completed task lists in the output
162	
163	## How it Works
164	
165	1. The server creates a `.tasks` folder in your repository root
166	2. Task lists are stored with descriptive filenames based on 2-3 word descriptions
167	3. When all tasks in a list are completed, the file is renamed with a ✅ prefix
168	4. The server parses the input text from Cursor's agent to extract tasks
169	5. It extracts tasks using various pattern matching techniques:
170	   - Numbered steps (1. First step)
171	   - Bullet points (* Item one)
172	   - Task-like sentences containing keywords like "should", "must", "need to"
173	6. If no clear tasks are found, it organizes the content into logical sections
174	7. The result can be tracked and updated as tasks progress
175	
176	## Example Workflow
177	
178	1. An agent proposes changes to your authentication system
179	2. You convert this plan to a task list: `convert_plan_to_tasks(plan_text, "Auth System Refactor", "auth-refactor")`
180	3. The tasks are saved to `.tasks/auth-refactor.md`
181	4. As you complete tasks, mark them: `mark_task_complete("auth-refactor", 1)`
182	5. When all tasks are done: `check_all_tasks_complete("auth-refactor")`
183	6. The file is renamed to `.tasks/✅auth-refactor.md`
184	
185	## License
186	
187	MIT 
188	

